<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arghun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1258–<a href="/years/1291">1291</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arghun was the fourth Mongol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, reigning from 1284 to 1291. He came to power by overthrowing his uncle Tekuder, and he sent several embassies to the pope and the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> seeking an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> he executed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II</a> in 1289 and treated the kingdom as a dependent territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arghun.jpg" alt="Arghun" width="287" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sayf al-Vâhidî · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArghunEnthroned_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arghun was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa Khan</a> and a concubine, Qaitmish Egeci, and was born on 8 March 1258 near Baylaqan, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-hamadani">Rashid al-Din</a> gives 1262, which is thought unlikely. He grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and at twenty commanded an army against the Negudaris. Like his father he was a Buddhist, though favourable to Christians.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> died in 1282 Arghun was talked out of contesting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a>, and his uncle Ahmad Tekuder was chosen. Arghun blamed the Juvayni brothers, who rose under Tekuder, for his father's death by poisoning, and he rebelled in 1283. He beat Tekuder's general Alinaq near Qazvin on 4 May but later surrendered to him. Buqa, a senior commander of Tekuder, freed Arghun, killed Alinaq and arrested Tekuder, who was handed to Arghun on 26 July 1284 and executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Arghun was informally enthroned on 11 August 1284. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> confirmed him on 23 February 1286, and a second coronation followed on 7 April 1286. Buqa, to whom he owed the throne, became both sahib-i divan and amir al-umara and was named chingsang. Shams al-Din Juvayni was executed. Arghun grew to resent Buqa and his brother Aruq, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> as his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>; he audited their finances, and when Buqa conspired against him, Prince Jushkab revealed the plot. Buqa was put to death on 16 January 1289, and King Demetre II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, who was implicated, was summoned to the capital and imprisoned.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sad-al-dawla">Sa'd al-Dawla</a>, a Jewish physician, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and restored order. Jushkab was executed on 10 June 1289, and Hulachu and Qara Noqai were executed in October 1290 after Nawruz's revolt. Arghun also fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, repulsing raids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> in 1288 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> in 1289, and founded the city of Arghuniyya near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Arghun sent four missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> seeking a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-mongol-alliance">Franco-Mongol alliance</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. The first, in 1285, carried a letter to Pope Honorius IV. In 1287 the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabban-sauma">Rabban Bar Sauma</a> went to the pope and to Edward I and Philip IV, and returned in 1288 with friendly but vague replies. Buscarel of Gisolfe went in 1289, and Andrew Zagan in 1290. In 1290 he also began a shipbuilding programme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, which failed after Genoese quarrels. The Mamluks took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> on 18 May 1291, after his death.</p><p>Arghun became interested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alchemy">alchemy</a>, and after taking a concoction from a yogi fell ill. He died in Arran in March 1291, on 7 or 10 March, and was buried in a secret place in the mountains of Sojas. The missionary Ricoldo of Montecroce called him a man given to the worst villainy yet a friend of the Christians. Children in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> were named Argone after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arghun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1290 the Ilkhan <b>Arghun</b> told Pope Nicholas IV that whoever prays to Eternal Tengri with righteous thoughts has done as much as if he had received baptism.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Arghuns</b>, Turko-Mongol nobles who had served the Timurid rulers of Herat, held Kandahar until Babur drove them out in the 1510s.</blockquote><a href="/state/arghun-dynasty">Arghun dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Jam Nizam al-Din, known as Jam Nindo, ruled for more than four decades around 1461 to 1508 and is remembered as a just and pious ruler who defended Sindh against the <b>Arghuns</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/samma-dynasty">Samma dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Mirza Isa Tarkhan, an officer of the Arghun dynasty descended from a Mongol military family, seized Thatta in 1554 as <b>Arghun</b> authority collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/tarkhan-dynasty">Tarkhan dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. Died in Arran, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1279" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7032 544 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arghun"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7482 814h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7482 814h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1279">What was here in 1279? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
